## The grammar's tasks {Tangle}[packages/tree-sitter-loom/moon.yml]

The tree-sitter grammar's test reads every loom in the repository with both the grammar and Loom's own classifier, and compares the two, line by line. So its sources are wider than a package's usual. They are the grammar itself, its scanner, its queries and its generated parser, the language and the AST that supply the classifier, and every `.loom` file in the book, the docs and the examples. A loom edited anywhere runs the test again, since the edit may reach a corner of the grammar no test has met.

The test needs two tools beyond Bun. The `tree-sitter` command arrives as a package, and a C compiler builds the parser, which every runner and every machine that builds Loom already has.

=>

$schema: "https://moonrepo.dev/schemas/project.json"

fileGroups:
  sources:
    - "grammar.js"
    - "src/**/*"
    - "queries/**/*"
    - "editors/**/*"
    - "test/**/*"
    - "package.json"
    - "tree-sitter.json"
    - "tsconfig.json"
    - "/packages/loom-ast/src/**/*"
    - "/packages/loom-ast/package.json"
    - "/packages/loom-lang/src/**/*"
    - "/packages/loom-lang/package.json"
    - "/packages/effect-test/**/*"
    - "/corpus/**/*.loom"
    - "/docs/**/*.loom"
    - "/examples/**/*.loom"
    - "/tsconfig.base.json"
    - "/package.json"
    - "/bun.lock"
    - "/bunfig.toml"

tasks:
  install:
    command: "bun install --frozen-lockfile"
    options:
      cache: false
      internal: true
      runFromWorkspaceRoot: true

  typecheck:
    command: "bunx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json"
    deps: ["install"]
    inputs:
      - "@group(sources)"
      - "moon.yml"
      - "/mise.toml"

  test:
    command: "bun test"
    deps: ["install"]
    inputs:
      - "@group(sources)"
      - "moon.yml"
      - "/mise.toml"

## The website's tasks {Tangle}[site/moon.yml]

The website builds on no package of the workspace. Its sources are its own code, its assets, the two pages and configs that build it, and the licence it shows on a page of its own. It type-checks twice, once as the client the browser runs and once as the server Bun runs, since the two compile under different configs.

The website also ships, as a container image, and the image task builds it. It builds `site/Dockerfile` with the whole repository as its context and tags the image three ways: `latest`, `sha-` with the commit's short hash, and `v` with the version the CLI's manifest carries. It then pushes every tag to the registry. A change that touches none of the image's inputs builds no image, so a fix to a package's tests leaves the website that is already running alone. The task runs in CI alone, so no developer's moon pushes an image, and moon never caches it, because what it makes lives in the registry rather than in any file.

=>

$schema: "https://moonrepo.dev/schemas/project.json"

fileGroups:
  sources:
    - "src/**/*"
    - "public/**/*"
    - "index.html"
    - "package.json"
    - "tsconfig.json"
    - "tsconfig.server.json"
    - "vite.config.ts"
    - "/LICENSE"
    - "/package.json"
    - "/bun.lock"
    - "/bunfig.toml"

tasks:
  install:
    command: "bun install --frozen-lockfile"
    options:
      cache: false
      internal: true
      runFromWorkspaceRoot: true

  typecheck:
    script: "bunx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json && bunx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.server.json"
    deps: ["install"]
    inputs:
      - "@group(sources)"
      - "moon.yml"
      - "/mise.toml"

  test:
    command: "bun test"
    deps: ["install"]
    inputs:
      - "@group(sources)"
      - "test/**/*"
      - "moon.yml"
      - "/mise.toml"

  image:
    script: >-
      docker build . --file site/Dockerfile
      --tag ghcr.io/athrio-com/loom-website:latest
      --tag ghcr.io/athrio-com/loom-website:sha-${GITHUB_SHA::7}
      --tag ghcr.io/athrio-com/loom-website:v$(node -p "require('./packages/loom/package.json').version")
      && docker push --all-tags ghcr.io/athrio-com/loom-website
    inputs:
      - "@group(sources)"
      - "Dockerfile"
      - "moon.yml"
    options:
      cache: false
      runInCI: "only"
      runFromWorkspaceRoot: true

## What the workflow answers to

A push to `main` checks what it changed and ships the website if the website changed. A pull request runs the checks alone, since an image from an unmerged branch has nowhere to go. A newer run on the same branch cancels an older one still going, because only the latest commit can be the one to ship. A cancelled run loses nothing: the next run measures its change from the last build that passed, so it takes the cancelled run's change on as well.

=>

name: CI

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  pull_request:

concurrency:
  group: ci-${{ github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: true

## The last build that passed

The commit a change is measured from depends on what carries the change. A pull request is measured from the commit it asks to be merged into. A push to `main` is measured from the last build that passed: the head of the latest run of this workflow on `main` that succeeded. Every change since that commit arrived either with this push or with a run that failed or was cancelled, and this run takes all of them on. Until a build has passed, the commit the push began from stands in.

Left to itself, moon would measure a push from the commit before its head. That misses changes: when `main` was merged into a branch before the branch was pushed, the merge's first parent is the branch's own last commit, and the branch's own work would build nothing. The last build that passed is the base that misses nothing. The first job asks GitHub for it before anything else and hands it to the second job, and it reads the repository and the workflow's own runs, nothing more.

=>

jobs:
  check:
    name: The book, typed, tested and tangled
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: read
      actions: read
    outputs:
      base: ${{ steps.base.outputs.sha }}
    steps:
      - name: The commit the change is measured from
        id: base
        env:
          GH_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}
          PULL_BASE: ${{ github.event.pull_request.base.sha }}
          PUSH_BEFORE: ${{ github.event.before }}
        run: |
          base=$PULL_BASE
          if [ -z "$base" ]; then
            base=$(gh api "repos/$GITHUB_REPOSITORY/actions/workflows/ci.yaml/runs?branch=main&event=push&status=success&per_page=1" --jq '.workflow_runs[0].head_sha // empty')
          fi
          echo "sha=${base:-$PUSH_BEFORE}" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"

## The gate, asked of the change

The first job checks the repository out with its whole history, which moon needs to find what changed between the two commits, and fetches a file's contents only when something reads the file. It gets Bun and moon from mise, at the versions `mise.toml` pins. It then asks `moon ci` for the five checks, measured from the base it found, one at a time: run side by side on the runner, the type checks and the tests slow each other down until a test misses its time limit.

=>

      - uses: actions/checkout@v7
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0
          filter: blob:none
      - uses: jdx/mise-action@v5
        with:
          install_args: bun npm:@moonrepo/cli
      - name: The book's checks, asked of the change
        run: moon ci --concurrency 1 :typecheck :test :tangle :nodesign :smoke
        env:
          MOON_BASE: ${{ steps.base.outputs.sha }}
          MOON_HEAD: ${{ github.sha }}

## Shipping the website

The second job runs for a push to `main` alone, once the first has passed, and measures its change from the same base. It reads the repository and writes packages, nothing else. It checks the repository out the same way, gets moon from mise, logs in to GitHub's registry with the token the run is given, and asks `moon ci` for the image. When the website did not change, moon builds nothing and the job passes.

=>

  ship:
    name: The website's image, pushed to the registry
    needs: check
    if: github.event_name == 'push'
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: read
      packages: write
    env:
      MOON_BASE: ${{ needs.check.outputs.base }}
      MOON_HEAD: ${{ github.sha }}
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v7
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0
          filter: blob:none
      - uses: jdx/mise-action@v5
        with:
          install_args: npm:@moonrepo/cli
      - name: Log in to the registry
        run: echo '${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}' | docker login ghcr.io -u '${{ github.actor }}' --password-stdin
      - name: The website's image, pushed to the registry
        run: moon ci :image

## The workflow {Tangle}[.github/workflows/ci.yaml]

=>

::[What the workflow answers to]

::[The last build that passed]

::[The gate, asked of the change]

::[Shipping the website]
